  {
    "subject": "biology",
    "question": "Why does a neuron fire an action potential?",
    "firstWordsMs": 3106,
    "speech": [
      "It all comes down to sodium rushing in. I’ll sketch the membrane to show you exactly how that spark starts. ",
      "When a neuron receives enough incoming signals, its membrane voltage rises toward a threshold around minus fifty-five millivolts. Once it hits that threshold, voltage-gated sodium channels snap open, allowing positively charged sodium ions to flood rapidly into the cell. This sudden wave of positive charge flips the internal voltage from negative to positive in an all-or-none event, which then propagates down the axon as the electrical impulse. Does that make sense, or would you like to explore what happens during repolarization?"
    ],
