  {
    "subject": "chemistry",
    "question": "Why does a catalyst speed up a reaction?",
    "firstWordsMs": 4470,
    "speech": [
      "A catalyst provides a different pathway for the reaction that requires less energy to get started. Let me sketch how that lowers the barrier for you.\n",
      "In any chemical reaction, reactant molecules must collide with enough kinetic energy to break bonds and reach the transition state. This minimum energy threshold is called the activation energy. \n\nWhen you add a catalyst, it offers an alternative reaction mechanism with a lower activation energy barrier. Because the barrier is lower, a much higher fraction of colliding molecules possess sufficient energy to react at that temperature, which makes the overall reaction proceed much faster without consuming the catalyst itself."
