  {
    "subject": "physics",
    "question": "How does a pendulum exchange energy?",
    "firstWordsMs": 4269,
    "speech": [
      "Let me show you how that energy swaps back and forth.\n",
      "A pendulum constantly trades gravitational potential energy and kinetic energy.\n\nWhen you pull the bob up to its highest point and release it, it pauses for an instant. At that peak, its speed is zero, so all of its mechanical energy is potential energy stored by gravity.\n\nAs gravity pulls it downward, the bob accelerates. Height drops while speed climbs, converting potential energy directly into kinetic energy. \n\nAt the very bottom of the arc, the bob is at its lowest point and highest speed. Here, potential energy hits minimum and kinetic energy reaches maximum.\n\nMomentum carries it past the bottom and back upward against gravity. It slows down as kinetic energy turns back into potential energy until it stops at the opposite peak, ready to swing back. \n\nIn an ideal system without air resistance or friction, the total mechanical energy remains constant throughout the entire swing. Does that balance make sense?"
    ],
