  {
    "subject": "matrices",
    "question": "What does a rotation matrix do to a grid?",
    "firstWordsMs": 3609,
    "speech": [
      "Let's see that rotation in action by drawing how it moves the grid.\n",
      "A rotation matrix turns every single point on the grid around the origin by a fixed angle, preserving all lengths and angles between grid lines.\n\nBecause matrix transformations are linear, what happens to the whole grid is entirely determined by where the standard basis vectors land. The unit vector along the x-axis rotates to coordinates given by cosine and sine of the angle, and the unit vector along the y-axis rotates to negative sine and cosine.\n\nEvery grid square pivots together as a rigid sheet, staying square and keeping the exact same area."
    ],
