    "subject": "calculus",
    "question": "What does a derivative mean?",
    "firstWordsMs": 2868,
    "speech": [
      "It’s basically the rate at which something is changing right now. Let me sketch out what that looks like.\n",
      "If you are driving a car, your average speed tells you how far you went over a whole hour, but your speedometer tells you your speed at this exact millisecond. That speedometer reading is the derivative.\n\nOn a graph, while average change is the slope between two distant points, the derivative zooms in so close that the curve looks like a straight tangent line at that single spot. Its slope is your derivative. \n\nDoes that distinction between average change and instantaneous change make sense?"
