  {
    "subject": "computing",
    "question": "How do packets travel across a network?",
    "firstWordsMs": 5470,
    "speech": [
      "Let's draw a network and watch some packets flow.\n",
      "When you send data like a message or video, your computer breaks it down into small chunks called packets. \n\nEach packet gets wrapped with a header containing metadata, such as the source IP address, destination IP address, and a sequence number.\n\nAs packets leave your device, intermediate routers read the destination address in the header. Each router checks its routing table and forwards the packet along the most efficient available link toward the next hop. Because network conditions change constantly, different packets from the same message can take entirely different routes to avoid congestion.\n\nOnce the packets reach the destination host, the receiving device uses the sequence numbers to reassemble them back into the original data stream, requesting retransmission for any packets that were lost along the way."
    ],
