    "firstWordsMs": 4987,
    "speech": [
      "Let me show you exactly how that pressure builds up and releases.\n",
      "Tectonic plates are massive slabs of rock that slowly move over the hot mantle below. Because their edges are rough and jagged, they cannot slide smoothly past one another. Instead, friction snags them and locks the fault in place while the rest of the plates keep moving. \n\nThis causes the rock around the fault to bend and store energy like a compressed spring. Once the accumulated stress exceeds the friction holding the rocks together, the fault suddenly snaps and slips. That rapid release of stored elastic energy radiates outward through the Earth as seismic waves, which is the shaking we feel as an earthquake. \n\nDoes this elastic rebound mechanism make sense, or would you like to explore the different types of plate boundaries?"
