    "question": "How does dramatic irony work in Romeo and Juliet?",
    "firstWordsMs": 5067,
    "speech": [
      "It's a powerful tool because the audience knows the tragic ending while the characters remain hopeful. I'll sketch out a quick example to show how that tension builds.\n",
      "Shakespeare gives the audience vital knowledge before the characters have it, turning normal choices into moments of intense suspense. \n\nFrom the very prologue, we are told that star-crossed lovers will take their lives. Because we carry that foreknowledge, every optimistic declaration of love carries an undercurrent of grief. \n\nThe peak happens in the final act: we know Juliet is only under the influence of a sleeping potion, but Romeo genuinely believes she is dead. When he notices her cheeks are still warm and flushed, the audience desperately hopes he will realize the truth before drinking the poison. \n\nThat gap between what we know and what the characters know is what makes the tragedy hit so hard. Does that make sense, or would you like to explore another specific scene?"
